  "notes": "The keyboard and bass hands behind much of Death Row, and the research is explicit that he is *not* the drum source — his workflow is to hear a beat and play over it. So this model is authored as harmony-first and deliberately as the complement of the Daz Dillinger entry, which covers the same sessions from the drum side: the plainest kit here relative to its neighbours (`syncopation` 0.44, straightforward kick placement, no doubled clap) under the richest keyboard block — minor 7th and 9th voicings at 0.62 combined extension weight, 2- and 4-bar cycles, and a chord `voicing.register` reaching 80 because layered keyboards spread. Bass is played on keys, melodic and syncopated at E1–D2 with slides between chord roots, which is a high `glideProb` over a narrow-ish register — the narrowest of the producer group, because the research gives an actual two-octave window. ⚠ `medium` on the research's high-medium. INFERRED: all densities and velocities. The documented habit that could not be modelled is the collaborative one — parts get mutually edited note-by-note in the room, so voicings are optimized for a specific drum pocket rather than generically, and the engine has no way to condition harmony on the drum part that was generated beside it.",
